<refentry id="gmime-filters">
  <refmeta>
    <refentrytitle>Stream Filters</refentrytitle>
    <refmiscinfo>GMime Library</refmiscinfo>
  </refmeta>

  <refnamediv>
    <refname>Stream Filters</refname>
    <refpurpose>How to use Stream Filters</refpurpose>
  </refnamediv>
  <refsect1 id="filter-overview">
    <title>Overview of Filters</title>
    <para>Stream filters are an efficient way of converting data from
    one format to another. To use a stream filter, you must first
    construct a GMimeStreamFilter stream and then add the desired
    filters to it.</para>

    <para>GMime comes equipped with some basic filters such as
    GMimeFilterBasic, GMimeFilterCharset, GMimeFilterDos2Unix,
    GMimeFilterUnix2Dos, GMimeFilterFrom and GMimeFilterHTML.</para>

    <para>The GMimeFilterBasic filter is actually a collection of
    filters for common transfer encodings used by MIME. So far, it is
    able to handle encoding and decoding of base64, quoted-printable
    and (x-)uuencode.</para>

    <para>GMimeFilterBasic's create_encoder() and create_decoder()
    factories accept a content encoding enum or name and return a
    GMimeFilterPassThrough for unhandled encodings. Encoder factories use
    CRLF by default; the explicit newline variants also accept a maximum
    line length. The existing new() constructor retains LF defaults and
    copying pass-through codecs.</para>

    <para>GMimeFilterPassThrough is a zero-copy identity filter. It
    returns the caller's input buffer, length and prespace unchanged,
    including on completion. Unlike a GMimeFilterBasic using a
    pass-through codec, it does not allocate an output buffer. Use it
    when an optional transformation should leave the stream untouched.</para>

    <para>GMimeFilterCharset converts complete input characters immediately
    with the native iconv backend, growing its output buffer as necessary.
    Incomplete input is retained until another call; completion discards an
    incomplete final character and emits any final target shift sequence.
    Reset discards both pending input and shift state. Copying creates a
    fresh converter with the same original source/target charset names,
    available through get_from_charset() and get_to_charset(). Native
    invalid/unrepresentable-byte skipping and iconv byte-order-mark policy
    are retained, rather than managed Encoding replacement fallbacks.</para>

    <para>GMimeFilterAnonymize replaces every byte except ASCII space,
    tab, CR and LF with lowercase ASCII 'x'. It preserves byte counts and
    newline spellings, including when input is split across calls.
    Input is never modified: output uses filter-owned storage. Completion
    adds no bytes and the filter can be reused without resetting it.
    This byte transformation alone does not anonymize message headers or
    MIME structure; original lengths and whitespace patterns remain.</para>

    <para>For internationalization support, GMime also includes a
    filter for converting between any 2 charsets,
    GMimeFilterCharset. With this filter, mail user agents can allow
    the user to read the message content in his or her own
    charset.</para>

    <para>A common conversion needed for text is CRLF -> LF and LF ->
    CRLF which is needed for most (all?) internet protocols. Luckily,
    GMime comes equipped with a set of filters to handle this for you:
    GMimeFilterDos2Unix and GMimeFilterUnix2Dos.</para>

    <para>On Unix systems, mail often resides in spools in mbox
    format. Mbox uses a line that starts with "From " to delimit
    messages which means that message bodies are forbidden to contain
    lines starting with "From ". The common way to escape these from
    lines in message bodies is to prepend the line with a greater-than
    character ('>') producing ">From ". You'll find GMimeFilterFrom
    handy when dealing with this.</para>

    <para>The GMimeFilterHTML filter converts a normal text stream
    into an html stream. This is especially useful if you are using a
    widget such as GtkHTML to display the contents of an email
    message.</para>
  </refsect1>
</refentry>
